      </div>
      <p class="img-caption">Body moisturiser formulations span from water-white lightweight lotions to rich ivory anhydrous butters — the physical appearance of the product directly reflects its water-to-lipid ratio and the corresponding differences in TEWL reduction, absorption rate, and skin type suitability.</p>

      <!-- SECTION 6 -->
      <section id="selection-guide">
        <h2>Formulation Selection: Skin Type, Climate, and Application Context</h2>
        <p>
          The formulation decision between body lotion and body butter is ultimately a skin-type and context-matching exercise grounded in the moisturisation mechanisms described above. Skin type — ranging from oily through normal to dry and very dry — determines the level of occlusion and emollient concentration the skin requires to maintain adequate hydration without feeling greasy or uncomfortable. Climate profoundly modifies this requirement: in cold, low-humidity environments, TEWL is elevated and the skin's own lipid barrier is stressed; in warm, humid climates, a lightweight, fast-absorbing vehicle is preferred because the ambient humidity provides partial humectant support and heavier products feel uncomfortable.
        </p>
        <p>
          For dry to very dry skin types — characterised by visible flaking, rough texture, tightness after washing, and elevated TEWL above 15 g/m²/h — body butter delivers clinically superior moisturisation outcomes because its 100% lipid content addresses both the occlusive and emollient deficits simultaneously at high concentration. Body butter is particularly effective applied immediately after bathing to damp skin, where the water film on the skin surface provides the humectant component that the anhydrous product itself does not contain. Normal skin benefits from the lightweight profile of body lotion, which delivers adequate emolliency through the dispersed oil phase while the continuous aqueous phase evaporates without leaving a heavy residue. Combination and oily skin types may find even standard body lotion too rich in some areas — very light, high-water-content lotions or gel-cream formulations (O/W emulsions stabilised with carbomer or acrylate copolymers) are more appropriate. As noted in the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Transepidermal_water_loss" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">clinical literature on TEWL assessment</a>, the measurable reduction in TEWL is the gold-standard efficacy metric for comparing moisturiser formats in clinical settings and in regulatory substantiation of moisturising claims under EU Cosmetics Regulation 1223/2009.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- FAQ -->
      <section class="faq-section" id="faq">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>What is the difference between a body lotion and a body butter?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">Body lotion is an O/W emulsion with 70–80% water as the continuous phase and dispersed oil droplets stabilised by emulsifiers. Body butter is anhydrous — 100% lipid phase with no water, no emulsification, and no antimicrobial preservation requirement. Body lotion is lighter, absorbs faster, and suits normal to combination skin; body butter is richer, more occlusive, reduces TEWL more effectively, and suits dry to very dry skin. The structural difference — emulsion vs anhydrous — drives all the practical differences in texture, absorption, preservation, stability challenges, and optimal skin type suitability.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>How does a body lotion emulsion stay stable?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">Emulsion stability is maintained by the emulsifier system (cetearyl alcohol + PEG emulsifier, or PEG-free alternatives), which reduces interfacial tension between oil and water phases and forms a protective layer around dispersed oil droplets that resists coalescence. Continuous phase viscosity builders (carbomer, xanthan, HEC) slow droplet movement and reduce creaming rate. Stability is validated through centrifugation, freeze-thaw cycling, and accelerated storage at 40°C for 3–6 months. Preservatives (phenoxyethanol, ethylhexylglycerin) protect the aqueous phase against microbial contamination throughout shelf life.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Why does body butter not need a preservative?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">Microbial growth requires water — bacteria, yeast, and mould cannot establish viable populations in an anhydrous environment with water activity below 0.6. Body butters contain no added water, making them intrinsically resistant to microbial contamination. They do require antioxidant protection against oxidative rancidity of their unsaturated fatty acid components: tocopherol (vitamin E) at 0.1–0.5%, optionally with rosemary extract, is the standard system. High-PUFA oils (rosehip, hemp seed) accelerate rancidity and require higher antioxidant loading or are excluded from formulations targeting long shelf life.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>What causes body butter to bloom or become grainy?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">Bloom and graininess result from polymorphic fat crystallisation — the stearic and palmitic triglycerides in shea, cocoa, and mango butter can exist in multiple crystal forms. Slow cooling or temperature fluctuations during storage cause recrystallisation into larger, coarser beta-crystals that are visible and felt as grains. Prevention: rapid cooling during manufacture, whipping to incorporate air and disrupt large crystal formation, and reformulating the butter-to-liquid-oil ratio to dilute the crystallising fraction. Temperature control during distribution (avoiding cycles above the butter's melting point) is essential to prevent bloom in finished products.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>How is the texture of body butter controlled in formulation?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">Texture is controlled by three variables: the ratio of high-melting butters (cocoa, mango — harder) to lower-melting butters (shea — softer), the proportion of liquid oil incorporated (more oil = softer, more spreadable), and processing conditions (whipping at 30–35°C produces a lighter, aerated, fluffy texture; controlled cooling sets a fine crystal network). Rapid cooling below 20°C locks in fine crystals. All oils must be blended into the fully melted butter base above 45°C — adding liquid oil to partially crystallised butter produces uneven, greasy texture that cannot be remedied without remelting.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>What is transepidermal water loss (TEWL) and why does it matter for moisturisers?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">TEWL is the passive diffusion of water vapour through the skin barrier, measured in g/m²/h. Healthy skin TEWL is below ~10 g/m²/h; dry skin, eczema, and compromised barrier conditions elevate TEWL, reflecting depletion of the ceramide-rich lipid bilayer of the stratum corneum. Moisturiser efficacy is measured by TEWL reduction: occlusives (petrolatum, butters) form films that impede TEWL; emollients replenish intercellular lipids; humectants attract water into the stratum corneum. Body butters reduce TEWL more than body lotions at equivalent application levels due to higher occlusive lipid concentration.</div>
        </details>
      </section>

